---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import WikiLayout from "../../../layouts/WikiLayout.astro";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<BaseLayout title="Your First Game: From Idea to Reality" activeNav="quest-logs">
  <WikiLayout title="Your First Game: From Idea to Reality" activeNav="quest-logs" activeSidebar="your-first-game">
    <div class="callout callout-info">
      <strong>🕹️ Step 3 of 4:</strong> Turning an idea into a finished, polished game requires structure, realistic planning, and regular self-care.
    </div>

    <h2>Choosing Your First Real Project</h2>
    <p>
      Select a simple genre for your first official game project. Avoid making online multiplayer games, massive open-world RPGs, or complex fighting games, as these require huge teams and years of work. Instead, choose small, mechanics-focused projects like 2D puzzle games, top-down arena shooters, or side-scrolling platformers where asset demands stay small.
    </p>

    <h2>Game Development Phase Checklist</h2>
    <p>
      Organizing your work into distinct development stages keeps production structured and manageable from start to finish.
    </p>

    <div class="table-wrapper">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Project Stage</th>
            <th>Primary Tasks</th>
            <th>Milestone Deliverables</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Stage 1: Concept</strong></td>
            <td>Define design pillars, draft core loop, set scope limits</td>
            <td>One-page design sheet, feature list</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Stage 2: Prototype</strong></td>
            <td>Code simple shapes (greyboxing), test basic jump/shoot mechanics</td>
            <td>Playable greybox build with zero art assets</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Stage 3: Production</strong></td>
            <td>Create art assets, add audio tracks, construct levels, build UI</td>
            <td>Feature-complete vertical slice build</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Stage 4: Polish &amp; QA</strong></td>
            <td>Test for software bugs, tweak control feel, adjust balance</td>
            <td>Stable release build, localization setup</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Stage 5: Launch</strong></td>
            <td>Submit store builds, launch marketing campaigns, post updates</td>
            <td>Live public release on digital stores</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Common Pitfalls to Avoid</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Building a Custom Game Engine:</strong> Spending months writing custom engine code instead of picking an existing engine like Godot, Unity, or GameMaker to build the actual game.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Polishing Visuals Too Early:</strong> Spending weeks making character art look perfect before testing if the underlying game loop is genuinely fun.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Developing in Secrecy:</strong> Hiding your game from external playtesters until launch day, missing early warnings about confusing controls or bad pacing.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Endless Feature Addition:</strong> Continuously adding new ideas mid-project instead of finishing and refining existing mechanics.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Grounding Expectations and Mindful Mental Health</h2>
    <p>
      Game development takes time, patience, and practice. Half of all indie releases on Steam earn less than $5,000 gross lifetime revenue. Setting modest financial expectations prevents disappointment and helps you focus on mastering the craft.
    </p>
    <p>
      Building games is a marathon. Take regular rest breaks, set consistent work hours, avoid overnight crunching, and celebrate small achievements like squashing a tricky bug or completing a new UI menu. Remind yourself that every completed game makes you a stronger, more skilled developer.
    </p>

    <div class="callout callout-tip">
      <strong>Next Quest:</strong> Ready to package and distribute your creation? Proceed to <a href={`${base}quest-logs/publishing-guide/`}>Publishing Guide: Reaching Your Audience →</a>
    </div>

    <div class="citations-section">
      <h3>References</h3>
      <ol class="citations-list">
        <li>Indie Game Sales Statistics 2026: 500-2000 Copies Median, <a href="https://www.steampageanalyzer.com/blog/indie-game-sales-statistics" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">https://www.steampageanalyzer.com/blog/indie-game-sales-statistics</a></li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </WikiLayout>
</BaseLayout>
